[
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-of-one-datom-costs-its-nodes"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-10 on main's pins (an LMDB store of 28k datoms, history kept, content-addressed nodes, the :sync writer; median of 31): ``d/transact`` of one datom costs 718k ticks, 3.06 MB and 2.9 ms; ``d/with`` of it 59k ticks warm (114k cold). By phase: the apply 59k (the write checks 1.8k of it); the flush of 19 index nodes 393k, of which hashing their addresses 368k; the commit id 41k; reading back the parent's commit-graph entry 14k; konserve's multi-assoc of 22 values 207k (serializing 86-93k, `key->store-key` 40k, zstd 11k, the LMDB transaction 18k). A commit costs about 30k ticks a node written: 19 nodes for one datom, 382 for 300. Should cost: about 6k ticks a node (one digest 1-2k, one encode of a leaf a few k) plus a fixed 10k: 120-150k ticks and under 100 KB at 19 nodes. What exists on branches not yet on main (Datahike ``jolt/pins-2026-11-11``, jolt-overlay ``jolt/hasch-uuid5-reads-the-digest-array``): hasch's Jolt path makes one digest call a sequence with the same ids, a Branch's address on Jolt is one SHA-256 of its children's fasl bytes, a Leaf's hash input is built from its datoms' fields: 431k ticks and 2.02 MB. And on Datahike ``jolt/a-node-is-serialized-once`` (unreviewed): the :sync writer hands its next commit the generation of the commit it wrote last, so the parent's commit-graph entry is not read back (13.5k to 0.3k ticks), and a leaf's address is taken from the leaf as stored, which also makes a deep `verify-chain` pass on leaves holding out-of-line values (it answered :mismatch before): 395k ticks. Measured 2026-11-10 through root's writer (the write answered after the LMDB commit, before the sync; main of 250-277k datoms, load 6-14, 40 writes): about 24 nodes and 27 keys a write; ``datahike.writer/transact-batch!`` 685-695k ticks, 3.7 MB, 5-6 ms; the apply 82k; the flush 240-290k, of which the out-of-line pass 139-186k (the store's collection generation read once per node holding a large value, 9 reads, and a value-bytes call per datom, 937 a write) and the address hashing about 100k; the commit id 28k; konserve's multi-assoc 280-330k (node serialization about 120k, store keys about 54k, zstd 26k); the LMDB commit about 0.35 ms of waiting; about 0.4 ms of thread hops to the writer and back; the sync, F_FULLFSYNC, 3.7-7 ms, on the flusher. Datahike ``jolt/out-of-line-costs-its-large-values`` reads the generation once a flush and walks only collection values: the out-of-line pass 89-110k, the write 597-614k. Left, largest first: the store write (tasks konserve.impl.defaults/a-value-already-serialized-is-stored-as-it-is and konserve.impl.defaults/a-store-key-costs-one-digest), the commit id (34k: hasch of the stored commit; the audit recomputes it from content, so what it is does not change without the audit), the apply (59k against an estimated 25k: id resolution and report building not yet split). Also measured: n itself is high because a content id scatters new entities across every index (303 datoms dirtied 350 leaves): the data-model question is with the owner. Proof when done: a cost proof of a one-datom commit by nodes written, on a store on disk, sizes by store depth."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
]
